1
00:00:00,980 --> 00:00:02,450
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:03,430
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:06,860
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:10,527 --> 00:00:12,360
TOBIAS COLDING: Last
time, one of the things

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:17,820
we did were we proved true
mean value inequalities.

6
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:26,080
So we proved true
mean value inequality.

7
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:27,610
And let me recall those.

8
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,980
And the first one was that--

9
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:45,720
so if you had a function f on
some interval from a to b into R

10
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:54,520
and this function
f is differentiable

11
00:00:54,520 --> 00:01:02,480
then it said that there
exists some x0 with--

12
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:07,440
x0 between strictly
between a and b so that

13
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:14,900
the derivative at x0 is equal
to the average of the function--

14
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:18,600
or the average of the
function at the endpoints.

15
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:22,620
So this is the average
on the endpoint divided--

16
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:26,000
so it's average over the
length of the interval.

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,000
So this is what the
mean value theorem said.

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,920
And then there was a
second mean value theorem,

19
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:39,640
and that was the Cauchy
mean value theorem.

20
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:41,330
So the Cauchy mean
value theorem.

21
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:57,650
And that was where you had
two functions, f and g,

22
00:01:57,650 --> 00:02:02,250
on this interval into
R. And they are both

23
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:04,030
assumed to be differentiable.

24
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:15,490
And then again, it was that
there exists some x0 strictly

25
00:02:15,490 --> 00:02:18,270
between a and b.

26
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:33,650
And if you take the derivative
at x0 of f times the value--

27
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:38,410
the difference in value
of g at the endpoints,

28
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:44,970
then this is equal to the
derivative of g at x0 times

29
00:02:44,970 --> 00:02:48,390
the difference in value
of f at the endpoints.

30
00:02:51,799 --> 00:02:54,225
All right, so that was the
Cauchy mean value theorem.

31
00:02:54,225 --> 00:02:55,850
So it was a little
bit more complicated

32
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:57,930
than the actual
mean value theorem

33
00:02:57,930 --> 00:03:01,890
because you had two
functions at the same time,

34
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:05,490
and the point here was that
you can arrange this exterior

35
00:03:05,490 --> 00:03:08,310
to work for both points--
for both functions.

36
00:03:08,310 --> 00:03:16,130
Let me just make an
observation, that if--

37
00:03:16,130 --> 00:03:17,810
so if we're looking at--

38
00:03:26,227 --> 00:03:27,810
so the Cauchy mean
value theorem seems

39
00:03:27,810 --> 00:03:31,030
like a more elaborate version
of the mean value theorem,

40
00:03:31,030 --> 00:03:37,770
and it really is because if
you suppose that g here was

41
00:03:37,770 --> 00:03:41,130
a function that is equal to x--

42
00:03:41,130 --> 00:03:48,010
so g of x is equal to x, I
have f here on the integral

43
00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:51,250
from a to b--

44
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:53,470
this is, again, differentiable.

45
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:03,380
Well, if the other
function was just

46
00:04:03,380 --> 00:04:06,680
this rather trivial
function g, of course,

47
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:09,420
g is also differentiable,
and we even

48
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:15,260
know that the derivative
of g at all points is 1.

49
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:19,100
So if you are now applying
the Cauchy mean value theorem,

50
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:34,460
then you say that by the
Cauchy mean value theorem,

51
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:42,940
there exists some x0 with
x0 strictly between a and b.

52
00:04:42,940 --> 00:04:45,940
And so that the
derivative of f--

53
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:48,300
so that's whatever that is.

54
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:51,460
That's the derivative
of f at x0.

55
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:54,760
The value of g at
these two endpoints

56
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:56,760
where g is just
a function of x--

57
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:59,900
so this is just b minus a.

58
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:04,940
And the derivative of g is 1.

59
00:05:04,940 --> 00:05:11,400
And then it's the difference
in value of f at the endpoints.

60
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:16,040
And so now, if you just
divide by b minus a-- so this,

61
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:19,660
of course, implies
that f prime of x0

62
00:05:19,660 --> 00:05:26,660
is equal to f of b minus f
of a divided by b minus a.

63
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:28,660
So in other words--

64
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:31,580
and you see, this
here gives you--

65
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:35,780
this gives you the more
standard mean value inequality.

66
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:38,500
So in other words, the
Cauchy mean value theorem

67
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:44,140
is more general than this
mean value inequality

68
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:46,180
because you recover this--

69
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:48,040
if you just let one
of the two functions,

70
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:50,220
it'd be just the
trivial function x.

71
00:05:57,150 --> 00:05:59,830
OK, so that was
just an observation.

72
00:05:59,830 --> 00:06:03,110
The second thing I
want to talk about

73
00:06:03,110 --> 00:06:06,810
is something we didn't
get to last time.

74
00:06:06,810 --> 00:06:11,350
And these are these--

75
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:14,220
L'Hopital's rule.

76
00:06:20,030 --> 00:06:22,770
And I'm just going to
state one version of it.

77
00:06:22,770 --> 00:06:27,010
There are two versions, and
they're both in the notes,

78
00:06:27,010 --> 00:06:29,750
but I will just
state one of them.

79
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:31,510
And so this is--

80
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:33,790
so you have-- so
the situation here

81
00:06:33,790 --> 00:06:38,910
is that you have two
functions, f and g.

82
00:06:38,910 --> 00:06:42,030
And they are defined
on some interval,

83
00:06:42,030 --> 00:06:50,030
say from a to b into R.
The interval does not

84
00:06:50,030 --> 00:06:51,710
include the endpoint.

85
00:06:51,710 --> 00:06:54,490
So these are differentiable.

86
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:08,150
And then we assume
that the limit here--

87
00:07:08,150 --> 00:07:13,670
we assume that g of x is--

88
00:07:13,670 --> 00:07:19,310
and not only g of x, but
also g prime of x, are not

89
00:07:19,310 --> 00:07:22,030
equal to 0 for all x.

90
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:34,390
And then we assume that the
limit here of g of x as x

91
00:07:34,390 --> 00:07:39,190
goes to a, that this
here is 0, and the same

92
00:07:39,190 --> 00:07:42,610
holds for the function f.

93
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:49,080
So this here.

94
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:51,680
And then what you're
interested in is--

95
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:54,840
what you're interested
in is that you're

96
00:07:54,840 --> 00:08:01,160
interested in looking at
f of x divided by g of x.

97
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:03,960
So this here makes
sense, g doesn't vanish.

98
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:05,620
So this here makes sense.

99
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:10,760
And you are interested in
looking at this fraction

100
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:16,600
here as x goes to a.

101
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:20,600
If x goes to a, then the
denominator goes to 0,

102
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:23,760
and the denominator
also goes to 0.

103
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:27,060
And the question is, does
it have a limit or not?

104
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:31,480
And so there's one more
assumption here that you need,

105
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:37,679
and that is that if
you're looking at the--

106
00:08:37,679 --> 00:08:55,280
so assume also that the limit
here as x goes to a of f

107
00:08:55,280 --> 00:09:02,360
prime of x divided by g
prime, assume that this exists

108
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:08,760
and is equal to some L.

109
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:11,480
And then the claim
is that then--

110
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:21,520
so the claim here is
that this fraction here,

111
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:26,720
f of x divided by g
of x, that this does

112
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:36,800
have a limit as x goes to a,
and that limit is actually L.

113
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:42,980
So this is one
version of this rule.

114
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:45,560
And there's another
one, very similar.

115
00:09:49,130 --> 00:09:51,405
OK.

116
00:09:51,405 --> 00:09:54,290
And so we want to prove this.

117
00:09:54,290 --> 00:09:56,690
So we are looking at--

118
00:10:01,410 --> 00:10:05,690
so we want to apply
the Cauchy mean value

119
00:10:05,690 --> 00:10:08,090
theorem to prove this theorem.

120
00:10:08,090 --> 00:10:12,610
And so we're looking
at an interval here.

121
00:10:12,610 --> 00:10:16,690
So you're looking at, say, y.

122
00:10:16,690 --> 00:10:22,630
So here, you have
a; here, you have x;

123
00:10:22,630 --> 00:10:27,970
and then here, you have
y; and here, you have b.

124
00:10:27,970 --> 00:10:30,050
And I'm now going to apply--

125
00:10:30,050 --> 00:10:31,560
so we will apply the--

126
00:10:34,490 --> 00:10:39,830
so apply the Cauchy
mean value theorem.

127
00:10:50,250 --> 00:10:55,890
But we are applying it
on this interval here.

128
00:10:55,890 --> 00:11:02,330
Between-- so y here
is less than x,

129
00:11:02,330 --> 00:11:04,970
but is strictly bigger than a.

130
00:11:04,970 --> 00:11:08,970
And this is x is
strictly less than x.

131
00:11:08,970 --> 00:11:12,210
So, the function
was differentiable

132
00:11:12,210 --> 00:11:14,170
on this open interval--
so of course,

133
00:11:14,170 --> 00:11:15,650
differentiable on
both functions,

134
00:11:15,650 --> 00:11:18,450
differentiable on
this closed interval.

135
00:11:18,450 --> 00:11:25,330
So we can apply it-- and so
we get that f prime at some

136
00:11:25,330 --> 00:11:44,220
intermediate point x0,
times g of x minus g of y,

137
00:11:44,220 --> 00:11:47,580
this is equal to g prime--

138
00:11:47,580 --> 00:11:50,580
again, x0 here is some
intermediate point,

139
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:53,620
it lie between these two.

140
00:11:53,620 --> 00:12:01,280
g prime at x0 times f of
x minus g of x-- g of y,

141
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:05,500
sorry, like that.

142
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:10,070
This is just applying the
Cauchy mean value theorem.

143
00:12:10,070 --> 00:12:10,570
Sorry?

144
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:16,160
What did you say?

145
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:18,360
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah,
sorry about that.

146
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:19,740
Thanks.

147
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:22,800
Yeah, thank you.

148
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:23,300
Right.

149
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:25,520
And so now, I just
divide-- of course,

150
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:32,260
this here is supposed to be a
non-zero, so I can just divide.

151
00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:48,540
So we have that f prime of
x0 divided by g prime of x0.

152
00:12:48,540 --> 00:12:49,880
And then I will--

153
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:52,460
I want to divide
by this, but I just

154
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:55,546
need to make sure
that this here is--

155
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:05,860
that I can divide by it, that
is not 0, this thing here.

156
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:06,960
So I have this.

157
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:22,140
Now, think about x as being
fixed and do this for all y.

158
00:13:22,140 --> 00:13:25,080
If I do this--

159
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:31,460
if I do this for y where y
is very, very close to a,

160
00:13:31,460 --> 00:13:35,210
this here becomes really,
really close to 0.

161
00:13:35,210 --> 00:13:40,230
This here is fixed and
it's not equal to 0.

162
00:13:40,230 --> 00:13:44,050
So this here is very close
to 0, this is not equal to 0.

163
00:13:44,050 --> 00:13:47,510
So this here, if y is
really close, if x is fixed,

164
00:13:47,510 --> 00:13:51,810
y is really close to a,
this is definitely not zero,

165
00:13:51,810 --> 00:13:53,410
so I can divide by it.

166
00:13:53,410 --> 00:14:16,310
So, for x fixed and y
sufficiently close to 0,

167
00:14:16,310 --> 00:14:27,830
then you have, then, g of x
minus g of y is not equal to 0.

168
00:14:27,830 --> 00:14:34,470
And so I get that f prime at
this intermediate point divided

169
00:14:34,470 --> 00:14:38,030
by g prime at the same
intermediate point

170
00:14:38,030 --> 00:14:48,510
is equal to f of x minus f of y
divided by g of x minus g of y.

171
00:14:48,510 --> 00:14:52,730
And now you see that's
y here goes to 0--

172
00:14:52,730 --> 00:14:58,390
so x is fixed still as y goes
to-- sorry, not to 0, but to a,

173
00:14:58,390 --> 00:15:01,250
this here goes to
0 by assumption,

174
00:15:01,250 --> 00:15:04,110
this here goes to 0,
so the whole thing goes

175
00:15:04,110 --> 00:15:07,590
to f of x divided by g of x.

176
00:15:11,430 --> 00:15:14,560
Now the thing is that if you--

177
00:15:18,510 --> 00:15:21,640
so this is for all x fixed.

178
00:15:27,010 --> 00:15:32,470
All right, so now if you--

179
00:15:32,470 --> 00:15:34,250
so now, we want to use--

180
00:15:34,250 --> 00:15:47,320
so now, since f prime at, say,
z divided by g prime at z,

181
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:51,640
since this fraction
here, as z goes to a,

182
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:55,400
this is assumed to go to L.

183
00:15:55,400 --> 00:16:02,760
So this means that given
epsilon greater than 0,

184
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:06,460
there exists a
delta greater than 0

185
00:16:06,460 --> 00:16:19,280
so that if z here is less
than a plus delta, then

186
00:16:19,280 --> 00:16:20,740
this fraction here--

187
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:27,720
then f prime of z
minus g prime of z is--

188
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:32,100
minus L is less than epsilon.

189
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:42,080
So now coming back here,
if you were starting with--

190
00:16:42,080 --> 00:16:45,540
so this here, again,
was for all fixed x.

191
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:52,140
You had this statement.

192
00:16:52,140 --> 00:16:57,880
And again, x0 was
between y and x.

193
00:16:57,880 --> 00:17:03,960
So now if you-- so
if this x here--

194
00:17:03,960 --> 00:17:17,720
if x is-- is delta close to the
left endpoint of the interval,

195
00:17:17,720 --> 00:17:22,119
then you would have that x0.

196
00:17:22,119 --> 00:17:26,819
But x0-- remember
that x0 is lying here.

197
00:17:26,819 --> 00:17:35,290
So since x0 would lie here,
this means that also, x0 here

198
00:17:35,290 --> 00:17:40,670
is in this interval here.

199
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,370
And so this means
that this thing--

200
00:17:46,370 --> 00:17:54,410
this number here you
get is L up to some--

201
00:17:54,410 --> 00:17:59,470
it's L up to some
error of epsilon.

202
00:18:02,250 --> 00:18:06,290
So this here is
almost equal to L.

203
00:18:06,290 --> 00:18:09,770
And so this means
that this limit here

204
00:18:09,770 --> 00:18:15,550
is almost equal to L
within an error of epsilon.

205
00:18:15,550 --> 00:18:20,690
So this proof-- this
version of L'Hopital's rule.

206
00:18:20,690 --> 00:18:24,250
And that's the second version,
and that's in the notes,

207
00:18:24,250 --> 00:18:28,370
but I'll skip it, actually,
for now just because there

208
00:18:28,370 --> 00:18:31,550
was-- from the last lecture,
and it's very similar to this.

209
00:18:31,550 --> 00:18:32,388
Yeah?

210
00:18:32,388 --> 00:18:37,453
STUDENT: Why did you deduce the
g x minus g y is not equal to 0?

211
00:18:37,453 --> 00:18:38,370
TOBIAS COLDING: Sorry?

212
00:18:38,370 --> 00:18:39,010
Why--

213
00:18:39,010 --> 00:18:41,510
STUDENT: Why would you
deduce x that g x minus g

214
00:18:41,510 --> 00:18:43,090
y is not equal to 0?

215
00:18:43,090 --> 00:18:44,250
TOBIAS COLDING: Yeah, OK.

216
00:18:44,250 --> 00:18:52,370
So this is because
g of x is supposed

217
00:18:52,370 --> 00:18:54,870
to be not equal to x for all x.

218
00:18:58,630 --> 00:19:01,650
And now I'm fixing x.

219
00:19:01,650 --> 00:19:03,410
So this here is not 0.

220
00:19:03,410 --> 00:19:07,330
Then I'm letting y go
to a, but then this

221
00:19:07,330 --> 00:19:11,270
here goes to 0 by assumption.

222
00:19:11,270 --> 00:19:13,770
So you see that
if you're fixing x

223
00:19:13,770 --> 00:19:18,010
and you just take out y
sufficiently close to a, then

224
00:19:18,010 --> 00:19:20,730
this denominator is
definitely not 0.

225
00:19:20,730 --> 00:19:23,290
You really want
to let this-- you

226
00:19:23,290 --> 00:19:28,830
want to let y goes to a because
then this here is eliminated.

227
00:19:28,830 --> 00:19:31,160
This is not part of the
statement, that g of y.

228
00:19:33,860 --> 00:19:36,540
And so, again, as
long as we started

229
00:19:36,540 --> 00:19:41,600
with an x that was
sufficiently close to y--

230
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:46,920
if x was in this interval, then
x0 is even further to the left,

231
00:19:46,920 --> 00:19:48,880
so this is also
in this interval.

232
00:19:48,880 --> 00:19:53,160
We don't know exactly what x0
is, but it is in that interval,

233
00:19:53,160 --> 00:19:56,730
and so this means that this is
strictly smaller than epsilon.

234
00:19:59,780 --> 00:20:01,570
OK.

235
00:20:01,570 --> 00:20:05,580
So another thing we
talked about last time

236
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:07,640
was this with the
Taylor expansion.

237
00:20:13,140 --> 00:20:15,940
In that all of
these things really

238
00:20:15,940 --> 00:20:20,540
came out of this
broader theorem.

239
00:20:20,540 --> 00:20:26,940
And that was also the case
for the Taylor expansion.

240
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:29,220
So you have a function f--

241
00:20:29,220 --> 00:20:32,840
so this is-- just to remind
you about the Taylor expansion.

242
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:45,500
So you have a function f.

243
00:20:48,460 --> 00:20:53,740
And let's say that it's defined
on some interval here into R.

244
00:20:53,740 --> 00:20:56,100
And the function is--

245
00:20:56,100 --> 00:21:02,780
so f here is k times
